summary.value:
This paper establishes theoretical guarantees for the optimization dynamics of Sobolev training for two-layer neural networks with finite width. The theory can be used in an idealized setting to explain the Sobolev acceleration phenomenon observed across various network architectures.

The reviewers expressed constructive yet mixed strengths and weakness comments on this work. They pointed out the merit of being a novel rigorous theoretical work for explaining the phenomenon of Sobolev acceleration from the optimization perspective.

The reviewers also raised several common weakness and questions on the restrictive theoretical settings:
- setting for theoretical analysis is too restrictive: input Gaussian data, neural network with one hidden layer, ReLU activation, etc.;
- limited comparison with advanced regularization techniques other than the $L^2$ training;
- deterministic initialization v.s. the standard random Gaussian initialization;
- implications or connections to sample complexity for training the Sobolev loss in existing literature;
- the practical relevance of Sobolev training on diffusion models with current theoretical architecture analyzed in this paper.
